---
// Interactive orbit map of the asteroid belt, after Step 8 of the MPC tutorial "Plotting Asteroid
// Orbits". Data: tools/export_orbit_map.py (public/data/orbitmap/belt.bin, src/data/orbitmap.json);
// physics: src/scripts/orbitmap/kepler.js, checked by tools/validate_orbit_map/.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageStatus from '../../components/PageStatus.astro';
import meta from '../../data/orbitmap.json';
import { RESONANCES, resonanceA } from '../../scripts/orbitmap/kepler.js';

const root = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const tutorial = 'https://docs.minorplanetcenter.net/tutorials/notebooks/mpc_tutorial_orbit_plotting/';
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en');
const c = meta.counts;
const aJ = meta.planets.jupiter.a;
const res = RESONANCES.map(([p, q]) => ({ name: `${p}:${q}`, a: resonanceA(aJ, p, q) }));
const disabled = true;   // controls are enabled once the orbit data have loaded
---
<BaseLayout title="The asteroid belt in motion" description="About 105,000 real asteroid orbits from the Minor Planet Center, in your browser: watch the belt move, turn with Jupiter to see the Hilda triangle and the Trojan clouds stand still, and find the Kirkwood gaps.">
  <section class="inner-hero rf-hero">
    <div class="content-width inner-hero-content">
      <p class="eyebrow">INTERACTIVE · THE ASTEROID BELT</p>
      <h1>The asteroid belt in motion</h1>
      <p>Every dot is a real asteroid from the Minor Planet Center's orbit catalogue: {fmt(c.total)} of them, placed where their orbits put them on {meta.epoch}. Move them through time, turn with Jupiter, and rearrange them to see the gaps that Jupiter has carved. Everything is computed in your browser.</p>
      <PageStatus page="teaching/asteroid-belt" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="site-content rf-prose om-page">
    <div class="content-width om-layout">
      <div class="om-figure">
        <div class="om-stage">
          <canvas id="om-canvas" role="img" aria-label={`Map of ${fmt(c.total)} asteroid orbits.`}></canvas>
        </div>
        <p class="om-status" id="om-status" aria-hidden="true"></p>
        <p class="visually-hidden" id="om-live" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <div class="om-controls" aria-label="Controls for the orbit map" role="group">
          <div class="om-row">
            <button type="button" class="rf-button" id="om-play" aria-pressed="false" disabled={disabled}>Play</button>
            <label class="om-slider">Year <output id="om-time-out" for="om-time">{meta.epoch.slice(0, 4)}</output>
              <input type="range" id="om-time" min="-50" max="50" step="0.1" value="0" disabled={disabled} />
            </label>
            <label class="om-select">Speed
              <select id="om-speed" disabled={disabled}>
                <option value="1">1 year a second</option>
                <option value="3" selected>3 years a second</option>
                <option value="10">10 years a second</option>
              </select>
            </label>
          </div>
          <div class="om-row">
            <fieldset class="om-radios">
              <legend>Frame</legend>
              <label><input type="radio" name="om-frame" value="stars" checked disabled={disabled} /> Fixed to the stars</label>
              <label><input type="radio" name="om-frame" value="jupiter" disabled={disabled} /> Turning with Jupiter</label>
            </fieldset>
            <fieldset class="om-radios">
              <legend>Show</legend>
              <label><input type="checkbox" id="om-show-0" checked disabled={disabled} /> <i class="om-key om-key-0"></i>Other orbits</label>
              <label><input type="checkbox" id="om-show-1" checked disabled={disabled} /> <i class="om-key om-key-1"></i>Hilda band</label>
              <label><input type="checkbox" id="om-show-2" checked disabled={disabled} /> <i class="om-key om-key-2"></i>Trojan band</label>
            </fieldset>
          </div>
          <div class="om-row">
            <label class="om-slider">View <output id="om-morph-out" for="om-morph">Space</output>
              <input type="range" id="om-morph" min="0" max="1" step="0.01" value="0" disabled={disabled} />
            </label>
            <button type="button" class="rf-button" id="om-reset" disabled={disabled}>Reset</button>
          </div>
          <p class="om-load" id="om-load">Loading {fmt(c.total)} orbits ({(meta.bytes / 1e6).toFixed(1)} MB)…</p>
          <noscript><p>The map needs JavaScript. The text alongside describes what it shows.</p></noscript>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="om-text">
        <h2>One snapshot</h2>
        <p>The map shows the inner Solar System from above, out to just beyond Jupiter. The white rings are the orbits of Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars and Jupiter. The dots are asteroids: every orbit in two narrow bands of semi-major axis that hold the Hilda and Trojan groups ({fmt(c.hilda)} and {fmt(c.trojan)} orbits), and a random {fmt(c.rest)} of the other {fmt(c.restAvailable)} orbits with semi-major axes below 6 au. Keeping the two small groups whole stops them being lost in the crowd.</p>
        <p>The broad ring between Mars and Jupiter is the main belt. Most known asteroids live there.</p>
        <p><button type="button" class="rf-button om-action" data-om-action="snapshot" disabled={disabled}>Show the snapshot</button></p>

        <h2>The belt in motion</h2>
        <p>Press Play and every asteroid moves along its own ellipse, the inner ones faster, as Kepler's third law requires: an orbit twice as large takes 2.8 times as long. Over a few decades the belt shears, while Jupiter goes round about once every 12 years.</p>
        <p><button type="button" class="rf-button om-action" data-om-action="play" disabled={disabled}>Play</button></p>
        <p>Now turn with Jupiter, so that the planet stays put. Two groups stop moving with it. The <b>Trojans</b> (orange) share Jupiter's orbit, in two clouds centred 60° ahead of and behind the planet, around the Lagrange points L4 and L5. The <b>Hildas</b> (pale) are in the 3:2 resonance: they go round three times for every two of Jupiter's. Each one reaches its farthest point from the Sun near L4, L5 or the point opposite Jupiter in turn, so at any moment together they trace a triangle.</p>
        <p><button type="button" class="rf-button om-action" data-om-action="rotate" disabled={disabled}>Turn with Jupiter</button> <button type="button" class="rf-button om-action" data-om-action="resonant" disabled={disabled}>Only Hildas and Trojans</button></p>

        <h2>Where the Kirkwood gaps hide</h2>
        <p>The main belt has gaps, first noticed by Daniel Kirkwood in the 1860s, at the semi-major axes where an asteroid would go round a whole number of times for every few of Jupiter's orbits. There, Jupiter's pull repeats at the same points of the orbit and, over long times, has stretched the orbits of asteroids and removed them. The gaps are invisible in the snapshot, because the eccentricity of each orbit spreads its asteroid over a wide range of distances from the Sun. Move every asteroid to its semi-major axis (across) and its eccentricity (up), and they open.</p>
        <p><button type="button" class="rf-button om-action" data-om-action="gaps" disabled={disabled}>Open the gaps</button> <button type="button" class="rf-button om-action" data-om-action="back" disabled={disabled}>Back to space</button></p>
        <p>The dashed lines mark where each resonance falls by Kepler's third law, with Jupiter at a = {aJ.toFixed(3)} au: an asteroid that makes p orbits for every q of Jupiter's has a = a<sub>J</sub>(q/p)<sup>2/3</sup>.</p>
        <div class="om-table-wrap">
          <table class="om-table">
            <caption>Resonances with Jupiter marked on the map</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Resonance (asteroid : Jupiter orbits)</th><th scope="col">Semi-major axis (au)</th><th scope="col">In the belt</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {res.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.name}</td><td>{r.a.toFixed(2)}</td><td>{r.name === '3:2' ? 'the Hildas, gathered in the resonance' : r.name === '1:1' ? 'the Trojans, sharing Jupiter’s orbit' : 'a gap'}</td></tr>)}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>How it is made</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Data.</b> The orbits are the osculating elements in the MPC's <a href="https://minorplanetcenter.net/iau/MPCORB.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MPCORB.DAT</a> (downloaded {meta.mpcorbFile}; {fmt(meta.catalogueOrbits)} orbits, {fmt(meta.atEpoch)} of them at the standard epoch {meta.epoch}). The Hilda and Trojan bands are chosen by semi-major axis alone ({meta.bands.hilda[0]}–{meta.bands.hilda[1]} au and {meta.bands.trojan[0]}–{meta.bands.trojan[1]} au), so they also contain some asteroids that are not in those resonances. The planets' orbits come from JPL Horizons at the same epoch.</li>
          <li><b>Method.</b> This follows Step 8 of the MPC tutorial <a href={tutorial} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Plotting Asteroid Orbits</a>: one dot per asteroid at the common epoch, the Hildas and Trojans kept in full, and Kepler's equation solved for every object at once. Here the elements are moved forward or back in time about the Sun alone (the two-body problem), and the frame turning with Jupiter rotates by Jupiter's mean longitude.</li>
          <li><b>Not an ephemeris.</b> Real orbits change: Jupiter and the other planets perturb them, and the Hildas and Trojans are held in place by Jupiter's gravity, which this map leaves out. Without it the triangle and the clouds slowly dissolve, so the time range stops at 50 years either side of the epoch. In the frame turning with Jupiter, 94% of the Hilda-band asteroids beyond 4.4 au lie within 25° of the triangle's corners at the epoch, 77% after 50 years, and 57% after 100 years (42% would mean no triangle at all).</li>
          <li><b>Checked.</b> The page's Kepler solver agrees with REBOUND's two-body integration to within 40 km for 400 sampled orbits at six times over ±50 years; storing the elements compactly moves the dots by a median of about 16,000 km, far less than a pixel. See <a href="https://github.com/matthewjohnpayne/matthewjohnpayne.github.io/tree/main/tools/validate_orbit_map" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tools/validate_orbit_map</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="rf-links">
          <a class="text-link" href={tutorial} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the MPC tutorial ↗</a>
          <a class="text-link" href={`${root}teaching/reference-frames/`}>Reference frames, interactively →</a>
          <a class="text-link" href={`${root}explore/`}>More to explore →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    import meta from '../../data/orbitmap.json';
    import { startOrbitMap } from '../../scripts/orbitmap/app.js';
    startOrbitMap(meta, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .om-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 28px; }
  .om-figure { min-width: 0; }
  .om-stage { background: #0b1926; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden; }
  .om-stage canvas { display: block; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 1; height: auto; }
  .om-status { font-size: 13px; color: var(--body); margin: 8px 0 0; min-height: 1.5em; }
  .om-controls { display: grid; gap: 12px; margin-top: 10px; }
  .om-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px 18px; align-items: end; }
  .om-slider { flex: 1 1 200px; display: grid; gap: 4px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--body); }
  .om-slider output { font-weight: 650; color: var(--ink); margin-left: 6px; }
  .om-slider input { width: 100%; accent-color: #128f9c; }
  .om-select { display: grid; gap: 4px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--body); }
  .om-select select { padding: 7px 8px; border: 1px solid #9fbcc4; border-radius: 4px; background: #fff; color: var(--ink); }
  .om-radios { border: 0; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px 14px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink); }
  .om-radios legend { font-size: 13px; color: var(--body); padding: 0; margin-bottom: 4px; width: 100%; }
  .om-radios label { display: inline-flex; gap: 6px; align-items: center; }
  .om-key { display: inline-block; width: 9px; height: 9px; border-radius: 50%; }
  .om-key-0 { background: #4fced2; } .om-key-1 { background: #a9dfe4; outline: 1px solid #7aaeb4; } .om-key-2 { background: #e9a160; }
  .om-load { font-size: 12px; color: var(--body); margin: 0; }
  .om-text { min-width: 0; }
  .om-text h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .om-action { margin: 0 6px 6px 0; min-width: 0; }
  .om-table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  .om-table { border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; margin: 6px 0 10px; min-width: 420px; }
  .om-table caption { text-align: left; font-size: 13px; color: var(--body); padding-bottom: 6px; }
  .om-table th, .om-table td { text-align: left; padding: 6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .om-layout { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 560px); gap: 48px; align-items: start; }
    .om-figure { position: sticky; top: 16px; order: 2; }
    .om-text { order: 1; }
  }
</style>
